  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nA strong (1031 MB) high over Lake Superior is helping produce rather \nbrisk northeast winds which have been ushering in cooler \ntemperatures and gradually lower dewpoints. A closed mid-upper level \nlow seen on water vapor imagery is moving into western IA early this \nafternoon. This upper low is expected to slowly weaken as it pushes \neast into northern IL by midday Tuesday. The unseasonably moist air \nmass that helped feed heavy rainfall totals over the weekend is \nbeing shunted off to the south of the area. However, cold mid-level \ntemps (around -15C) associated with this weakening upper low should \nbe enough to result in scattered showers this afternoon across IA. \nThis shower activity is expected to attempt to spread east into \nnorthern IL late tonight into Tuesday morning as the upper low \npushes this way, however, the showers are expected to encounter a \ngradually drier air mass as they move east of the MS River. Have \nmaintained some 20-30% chances for showers late tonight into Tuesday \nAM, especially across our western CWA. \n\nThis shortwave trough is progged to continue eastward toward the \nEast Coast by Wednesday into Wednesday night, likely aiding in \nintense coastal cyclogenesis. A Rex Block is expected to develop \nover southeastern Canada into the Great Lakes later this week as a \nstrong, large cut-off coastal cyclone becomes quasi-stationary to \nthe south of a strong mid-upper high over southeastern Canada. For \nour area, this should result in an extended period of somewhat \ncooler temperatures and very little threat of precipitation from mid \nweek through the upcoming weekend. One small possible exception to \nthe dry weather could be Wednesday morning into the Chicago Metro \narea when there could be a few sprinkles or very light lake effect \nrain showers. Thermodynamics are marginal and with fairly dry low \nlevel air mass, the chances appear pretty small so didn't introduce \nany precip into the grids at this time, but did nudge pops up to \naround 10%. Otherwise, look for temperatures that will likely slowly \nmoderate late in the week into the weekend with temperatures \nprobably warming back into the 70s.\n\n- Izzi",
